@didikunz 
 > As I understand it, the step model is more in a linera fashion. What you show here can be represented with custom commands.

Just to be sure, you're referring to the customActions field right? Makes sense, thanks! Doesn't seem like you can limit custom actions to a certain step from an API point of view, right?

What about this:

```
    "thumbnails": [
        {
            "ratio": "16by9",
            "file": "../thumbs/16by9.png"
        }, {
            "ratio": "9by16",
            "file": "../thumbs/9by16.png"
        }, {
            "ratio": "1by1",
            "file": "../thumbs/1by1.png"
        } 
    ]
```

We could define a bunch of predefined aspect ratios. I guess there are less than actual resolutions. The images could be as big as the renderer the template runs on (1920x1080 etc.) or as small as the author see fit. The whole thing ("thumbnails" object) could be optional. Also the ratio could be optional, but then it would not make much sense to have more than one entry in the array.

I think we should include the `CEF`-version in the version descriptor, to allow for potential other rendering engines in the future.
Something like this, perhaps?
```json
{
    "renderRequirements": [
        {
            "engine": {
                "cefVersion": { "min": 139 }
            }
        }
    ]
}
```

What about:
```
{
    "renderRequirements": [
        {
            "engine": {
                "type": "CEF",
                "version": { "min": 139 }
            }
        }
    ]
}
```
Would allow other engines, like Gecko etc.

The original proposal would do so as well, when used like so:
```json
{
    "renderRequirements": [
        {
            "engine": {
                "cefVersion": { "min": 139 },
                "geckoVersion": { "min": 106 }
            }
        }
    ]
}
```
That said, I would still prefer @didikunz 's version, since it is a bit more verbose.

Or a combination of both:

```
{
    "renderRequirements": [
        {
            "engine": [
				{
					"type": "CEF",
					"minVersion": 139
				},
				{
					"type": "Gecko",
					"minVersion": 106
				}
			]
        }
}
```

The version could always be a minimal version, so that could be shorthanded, or could left as is for compatibility with other numeric values, that have a range etc. In this way different engines could be mention, without having fixed assomptions on types. So it could also be vendor specific types.

What we discussed during the meeting on 2025-10-29:

```
{
    "renderRequirements": [
        {
            "engine": [
				{
					"type": "CEF",
					"version": { "min": 139 }
				},
				{
					"type": "Gecko",
					"version": { "min": 106 }
				}
			]
        }
}
```

body:
## Proposal:

Add a `useInName?: boolean` property to the manifest schema properties. When `useInName = true`, this indicates that the value of the property should be included when labelling a graphics in a GUI.

## Motivation:

When working on implementing OGraf support into [Sofie TV Automation](https://sofie-automation.github.io/), I found that this would have been nice to have, in order to display meaningful labels to the user (and omit not-so-useful data).

### Example:

```json
{
    "$schema": "https://ograf.ebu.io/v1/specification/json-schemas/graphics/schema.json",
    "id": "title",
    "name": "Title screen",
    "main": "title.js",
    "schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            "mainText": {
                "type": "string",
                "title": "Main Title text",
                "useInName": true
            },
            "titleColor": {
                "type": "string",
                "default": "#d32f2f"
            },
            "titlePosition": {
                "type": "string"
            },
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, `mainText` would be useful to display in a gui (eg "Title screen: Johan Nyman"), whereas the color and position is not.

(The suggestions below are intended to spark discussion, perhaps we can come up with something even better?)

## Background

There are some graphics that take position data as input. One example is this one from the olympics:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/93cae12f-034f-4755-947c-83e4caba6f22)


## Relative position

Below are suggestions for how relative positions could be defined.
I include a `typicalValue`, with the intention that it is used to scale the input, so that "one human interaction" (like "one scroll", "one drag operation" or "one swipe") typically result in sent values with a sum in this magnitude.

```json
{
  "type": "number",
  "gddType": "relative-position-1d",
  "gddOptions": {
    "typicalValue": 100 // [Optional, number] A typical value during one interaction (An interaction can be a drag, or a scroll, etc).
  }
}
```
```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "gddType": "relative-position-2d",
  "gddOptions": {
    "typicalValueX": 100, // [Optional, number] A typical value during one interaction (An interaction can be a drag, or a scroll, etc).
    "typicalValueY": 100 // [Optional, number] A typical value during one interaction (An interaction can be a drag, or a scroll, etc).
  },
  "properties": {
    "x": {
      "type": "number",
    },
    "y": {
      "type": "number",
    }
  }
}
```
```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "gddType": "relative-position-3d",
  "gddOptions": {
    "typicalValueX": 100, // [Optional, number] A typical value during one interaction (An interaction can be a drag, or a scroll, etc).
    "typicalValueY": 100, // [Optional, number] A typical value during one interaction (An interaction can be a drag, or a scroll, etc).
    "typicalValueZ": 100 // [Optional, number] A typical value during one interaction (An interaction can be a drag, or a scroll, etc).
  },
  "properties": {
    "x": {
      "type": "number",
    },
    "y": {
      "type": "number",
    },
    "z": {
      "type": "number",
    }
  }
}
```

## Absolute position

Below are suggestions for how absolute positions could be defined.
The min/max values should be used by a GUI to define the values sent.


```json
{
  "type": "number",
  "gddType": "absolute-position-1d",
  "gddOptions": {},
  "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
  "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
  "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
  "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
}
```

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "gddType": "absolute-position-2d",
  "gddOptions": {},
  "properties": {
    "x": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    },
    "y": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    }
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "gddType": "absolute-position-3d",
  "gddOptions": {},
  "properties": {
    "x": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    },
    "y": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    },
    "z": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    }
  }
}
```

### Absolute positions - variants

#### GPS position

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "gddType": "absolute-position-2d/gps", // X and Y are longitude and latitude, respectively.
  "gddOptions": {},
  "properties": {
    "x": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    },
    "y": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    }
  }
}
```

#### Position on screen

(This would be used for the example screenshot above)

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "gddType": "absolute-position-2d/screen", // X and Y are positions on a screen with (0,0) being the top-left corner of the screen.
  "gddOptions": {},
  "properties": {
    "x": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    },
    "y": {
      "type": "number",
      "maximum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMaximum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "minimum": number,          // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
      "exclusiveMinimum": number, // [Optional] See JSON-schema definition
    }
  }
}
```

